/srv/claude/research/motor-unit-representation/pinned/orchestrating-ai-research-teams.v1.0-1ce8d1b3e42cfbec2122468c595d52d238d6ca1dfd079292b7d61d95fb3d11f7.md:571:### B.3 What a valid necessity claim looks like
/srv/claude/research/motor-unit-representation/pinned/orchestrating-ai-research-teams.v1.0-1ce8d1b3e42cfbec2122468c595d52d238d6ca1dfd079292b7d61d95fb3d11f7.md:579:5. **Ban:** benchmark wins alone must never prove necessity—scores support sufficiency only
/srv/claude/research/motor-unit-representation/pinned/orchestrating-ai-research-teams.v1.0-1ce8d1b3e42cfbec2122468c595d52d238d6ca1dfd079292b7d61d95fb3d11f7.md:588:A necessity claim is not fixed at the start, and it is not endlessly fluid. Each claim is in exactly one of three states:
/srv/claude/research/motor-unit-representation/pinned/orchestrating-ai-research-teams.v1.0-1ce8d1b3e42cfbec2122468c595d52d238d6ca1dfd079292b7d61d95fb3d11f7.md:611:| **Principle track** | Premise → necessity claim → grade (1/2/3) → scope of validity | May **veto** lines of work justified only by phenomena |
/srv/claude/research/motor-unit-representation/pinned/orchestrating-ai-research-teams.v1.0-1ce8d1b3e42cfbec2122468c595d52d238d6ca1dfd079292b7d61d95fb3d11f7.md:612:| **Phenomenology / baseline track** | Performance, demos, correlations | Proves sufficiency only; cannot alone promote a claim to necessity |
/srv/claude/research/motor-unit-representation/pinned/orchestrating-ai-research-teams.v1.0-1ce8d1b3e42cfbec2122468c595d52d238d6ca1dfd079292b7d61d95fb3d11f7.md:626:The aim is not to pretend biology-grade Newtonian laws already exist. The aim is to **maintain an explicit necessity ladder**, upgrade what can be upgraded, and forbid theorem-style wording for what cannot—thereby **shrinking the trial-and-error space**.
/srv/claude/research/motor-unit-representation/pinned/orchestrating-ai-research-teams.v1.0-1ce8d1b3e42cfbec2122468c595d52d238d6ca1dfd079292b7d61d95fb3d11f7.md:651:3. **Planning order is not evidence.** The human lead may adopt a ladder as a planning order. That is a brief constraint, scoped to the run or program. It binds the work but is not evidence, and a proposal may not cite the ladder's order as a necessity argument.  
/srv/claude/research/motor-unit-representation/pinned/orchestrating-ai-research-teams.v1.0-1ce8d1b3e42cfbec2122468c595d52d238d6ca1dfd079292b7d61d95fb3d11f7.md:652:4. **Passing a gate is sufficiency only.** A stage gate (a passed spike) is sufficiency evidence about that stage. It never establishes the necessity of the next edge and never changes a grade.  
/srv/claude/research/motor-unit-representation/pinned/orchestrating-ai-research-teams.v1.0-1ce8d1b3e42cfbec2122468c595d52d238d6ca1dfd079292b7d61d95fb3d11f7.md:666:5. **Gates before ranking.** Appendix B gates apply before rubric ranking (§4 Step 4). A rubric row about necessity discipline may score the quality of reasoning, but it never substitutes for the gate.  
/srv/claude/research/motor-unit-representation/pinned/orchestrating-ai-research-teams.v1.0-1ce8d1b3e42cfbec2122468c595d52d238d6ca1dfd079292b7d61d95fb3d11f7.md:677:- 2026-10-23: Appendix B — Principle necessity protocol (necessity grades, principle versus performance tracks, minimal claim artifacts).
/srv/claude/research/motor-unit-representation/pinned/orchestrating-ai-research-teams.v1.0-1ce8d1b3e42cfbec2122468c595d52d238d6ca1dfd079292b7d61d95fb3d11f7.md:678:- 2026-10-23: Appendix B.4 — claim lifecycle (Candidate, Locked, Challenged). Scores may open a challenge; they do not promote or rewrite a lock.
/srv/claude/research/motor-unit-representation/pinned/orchestrating-ai-research-teams.v1.0-1ce8d1b3e42cfbec2122468c595d52d238d6ca1dfd079292b7d61d95fb3d11f7.md:679:- 2026-10-23: **v1.0.** Version header and pinning (entries above are v0). New §10: method issues, freeze set, amendment classes 0–3, contest-neutrality test, run retrospective. New §11: enforced isolation, seal and publish-after-all, Orchestrator–author firewall, prompt parity, conditions under which agreement counts. New §12: write-permission matrix, Experiment-runner code bounds, mechanical deposits and transcripts, append-only logs. New §13: program ledger, inheritance by ID, provisional cap, dependency propagation, pause/resume, step budgets. §4 Step 4: gates before ranking, independent and order-swapped adjudication, tie definition, Confounds paragraph. §6 rules 7–9. Appendix A: Orchestrator–adjudicator contradiction resolved, A.3 rules 6–7, new A.5 roster contract (model, effort, tools, parity). Appendix B: operational Grade-2 realization, challenge admissibility, new B.8 (capability ladders) and B.9 (claim governance). Motivated by process defects found in a live run: isolation enforced only by prompt, failure evidence overwritten on retry, an unversioned brief amendment, adjudicator role conflicts, and unequal author resources.
