/srv/claude/research/motor-unit-representation/logs/B01-step2-authorB-attempt1.log:431:88:- **Eligibility before ranking.** Apply the gates first. A hard-constraint violation, or a conflict with a Locked Grade-1 claim, means **Reject**. A missing Locked Grade-2 role means **Incomplete**, which is not eligible for Recommend (Appendix B.5). The rubric ranks only eligible candidates. Scores on other rows never offset a gate failure.  
/srv/claude/research/motor-unit-representation/logs/B01-step2-authorB-attempt1.log:432:178:Treat AI agents as **parallel researchers plus a strict reviewer**, not as a committee that must agree. The human lead owns the brief and the final signature. Competition and rubrics create pressure; short experiments discard elegant nonsense. With that rhythm, large scientific questions can converge instead of diverging forever in shared chat. Appendix B states how to judge a claim by necessity, so a strong score cannot be promoted into a locked principle. Sections 10–13 cover real runs: versioning and mid-run amendment of the method itself, enforced isolation in shared workspaces, who may write which artifact, and programs that span many packets and wall-clock limits.
/srv/claude/research/motor-unit-representation/logs/B01-step2-authorB-attempt1.log:433:287:### 11.5 When agreement counts
/srv/claude/research/motor-unit-representation/logs/B01-step2-authorB-attempt1.log:435:492:The brief-first rubric, blind review, minimal spike, single source of truth, and human final authority remain exactly as in the main methodology. Heterogeneity only binds *who* fills each role, and with what settings (A.5). Appendix B adds a Principle track with its own roles, bound in B.9. That track is an addition to the cycle, not a replacement for any step.
/srv/claude/research/motor-unit-representation/logs/B01-step2-authorB-attempt1.log:436:520:## Appendix B — Principle necessity protocol
/srv/claude/research/motor-unit-representation/logs/B01-step2-authorB-attempt1.log:437:522:This appendix is about **judging claims by necessity**, not by benchmark score. The goal is to extract constraints that can **rule routes in or out before the fact**—closer to physical laws and mathematical theorems—rather than justifying a technique only because some implementation performed well.
/srv/claude/research/motor-unit-representation/logs/B01-step2-authorB-attempt1.log:438:528:| | **Phenomenological / performance judgment** | **Principle / necessity judgment** |
/srv/claude/research/motor-unit-representation/logs/B01-step2-authorB-attempt1.log:439:531:| Force | “This can work” (sufficiency) | “That path is blocked / any solution must …” (necessity) |
